-There is no Steam Runtime sysroot for ARM64, so the build uses the system's
-own headers and libraries. ArmadaOS is image-based and ships without
-compilers, so the build runs inside a **distrobox** of the same Fedora
+There is no Steam Runtime sysroot for ARM64. When
+`config/release-toolchain.json` pins an ARM64 sysroot (an Ubuntu 24.04 arm64
+root with GCC 14's libstdc++ and the X11, Wayland and audio development
+files), setup downloads it like the other tools and the device needs only
+glibc, `git` and Python 3.11 or newer. Without one, the build uses the
+system's own headers and libraries; ArmadaOS is image-based and ships
+without them, so that build runs inside a **distrobox** of the same Fedora
 release, and the game then runs on the host.
 
+## With the launcher
+
+A Linux ARM64 launcher archive (`PinyonShift-Launcher-linux-arm64.tar.gz`)
+works as on the Steam Deck: unpack it in Desktop Mode, start
+`PinyonShift/PinyonShiftLauncher`, choose your ISO and keep the device on its
+charger. Setup downloads LLVM, CMake, Ninja and the pinned sysroot into the
+launcher's install folder (`~/.local/share/PinyonShift`), builds the game
+there and starts it with **Play**. Nothing is installed on the system.
+
 ## Build in a distrobox
 
 In Desktop Mode, open Konsole:
